Widen isRegionalIndicator() to char32_t

The helper tests for the Regional Indicator Symbols at U+1F1E6..U+1F1FF, but
takes its argument as wchar_t. wchar_t is 32 bits on Linux, so the comparison
works there by accident; on Windows it is a 16-bit unsigned short and neither
bound is representable, so the compiler folds the whole test to false and warns:

    lib/Screen.cpp:673:15: warning: comparison is always false due to limited
    range of data type [-Wtype-limits]

char32_t is the type the constants actually call for and is 32 bits
everywhere, which makes the comparison mean the same thing on every platform.
Both call sites pass a wchar_t and convert implicitly.

Note this only fixes the predicate's own arithmetic. qtermwidget stores each
cell's code point in a wchar_t (Character::character), so on Windows the
astral-plane value has already been truncated by the time it gets here and flag
emoji still will not compose -- that needs a wider Character, which is a much
larger upstream change.

Upstream: not submitted
Applies-to: all environments

--- a/lib/Screen.cpp
+++ b/lib/Screen.cpp
@@ -668,7 +668,7 @@
         clearSelection();
 }
 
-static inline bool isRegionalIndicator(wchar_t c)
+static inline bool isRegionalIndicator(char32_t c)
 {
     return (c >= 0x1F1E6 && c <= 0x1F1FF); // for creating flag codes
 }
